#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186599265 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "296d9194-b755-55fb-a920-e94099d3c308" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{2}}{1}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}=4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186598774} {:answer/id #uuid "3864cff4-57e5-51c4-98fc-8b92da615a99" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}=4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186598805} {:answer/id #uuid "44b9d501-b27e-5517-ba29-49aaa49c85b5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{1}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186598823} {:answer/id #uuid "b03bfd72-19a8-5191-9b62-fb303c9c7f59" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{2}}{1}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186598988} {:answer/id #uuid "d7f50e7e-c861-5a6e-84be-4bed6310b486" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{1}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186599051}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186599051} :answer-field/id #uuid "7e613d2f-b159-5f7b-82c3-d670add737d8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186599159}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "f6e1505f-d95c-5726-a4ab-ec96ef6d05f8" :question/math-academy-id "q-115107" :question/problem "![](images/93/93483ece115081ec99c6a844c06b27b2927dba1e23f7ee9bd3da7d3fed924aff.png)\n\nWhat is the equation of the ellipse given above?" :question/worked-solution "Remember that the equation of an ellipse centered at the origin with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\n \n\nHere, the horizontal radius has length $a=2$ and the vertical radius has length $b=1.$ Therefore, the equation of the ellipse is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{b}^{2}} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{{2}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{1}^{2}} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{1} & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186599774 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6846e12a-8c27-53f7-8cfe-bb746a3cf036" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$6$ and $3$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186599475} {:answer/id #uuid "a6daa5a1-9f46-511d-99a0-5839ee6e3bbb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$12$ and $6$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186599556} {:answer/id #uuid "ae65ebc5-dcc2-5834-8f9b-6661ff467013" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$36$ and $9$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186599565} {:answer/id #uuid "e99d4f63-e744-5aa6-b59d-778174054f20" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$24$ and $12$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186599642} {:answer/id #uuid "f0b1840a-a9bb-5845-addc-8fb4d2ddd66e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$12$ and $3$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186599653}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186599556} :answer-field/id #uuid "ab8a5745-cdc4-594d-9a94-2eb77843cfae" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186599737}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "0a7a3dbd-70b7-5aa3-861d-770dbf259134" :question/math-academy-id "q-115131" :question/problem "What are the lengths of the major and minor axes of an ellipse with equation \n$\\frac{{x}^{2}}{9}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{36}=1?$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that the equation of an ellipse centered at the origin with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn the given equation, we have ${a}^{2}=9$ and ${b}^{2}=36.$\n\n- Since ${a}^{2}=9,$ the length of the horizontal radius must be $a=\\sqrt{9}=3.$\n\n- Since ${b}^{2}=36,$ the length of the vertical radius must be $b=\\sqrt{36}=6.$\n\nThus, the semi-major axis has length $b=6,$ and the semi-minor axis has length $a=3.$\n\nTherefore, the major axis has length $2b=2\\cdot 6=12,$ and the minor axis has length $2a=2\\cdot 3=6.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186599781 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3059ff13-bee0-5604-921f-0e7150091621" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{2}}{49}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{16}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186599398} {:answer/id #uuid "59525aae-47bc-51bd-8b6e-5568cabc2862" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{2}}{7}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186599455} {:answer/id #uuid "595bbf9a-eb73-5c4c-ad57-6efce8a52e97" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{2}}{8}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{14}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186599456} {:answer/id #uuid "d4607d17-5d8c-5d6b-b901-2c80beea4276" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{7}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186599611} {:answer/id #uuid "d60378b8-9add-5f16-bf03-a06da9a95ffa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{2}}{16}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{49}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186599614}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186599614} :answer-field/id #uuid "dd545641-b01d-5f8f-9b58-dad5d22ad2fc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186599756}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "29587bc0-bd52-5b45-9596-e43bc1394444" :question/math-academy-id "q-115128" :question/problem "![](images/78/78fad41612540c2741146a412da7cd5988180f192b177af7192bbf72d842030f.png)\n\nWhat is the equation of the ellipse given above?" :question/worked-solution "Remember that the equation of an ellipse centered at the origin with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\n \n\nHere, the horizontal radius has length $a=4$ and the vertical radius has length $b=7.$ Therefore, the equation of the ellipse is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{b}^{2}} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{{4}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{7}^{2}} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{16}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{49} & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186599812 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "36783e09-69bb-52d2-8ee1-4e94b92f3bf5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{C}(xz+y)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186599407} {:answer/id #uuid "5b9103b3-b522-5132-913c-775d1094ab8a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{C}4xyz\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186599458} {:answer/id #uuid "5d7963ef-7f06-5f9a-b16c-4db640e7b00c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{C}(4+xz+y)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186599460} {:answer/id #uuid "bcab5fc9-1cc1-56d3-8686-efe8c1101a87" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{C}4\\,\\text{d}x+xz\\,\\text{d}y+y\\,\\text{d}z" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186599587} {:answer/id #uuid "dd89d545-3438-542d-8b07-053c1533e325" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{C}xz\\,\\text{d}y+y\\,\\text{d}z" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186599624}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186599587} :answer-field/id #uuid "2f254161-688f-5ae9-8530-70555d5bb001" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186599692}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "a0d71f86-74a8-5781-8c0d-fc2f3aa1a462" :question/math-academy-id "q-125415" :question/problem "Let $F(x,y,z)=4\\,i+xz\\,j+y\\,k$ and let $C$ be a path in ${R}^{3}.$ Which of the following is equivalent to $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r?\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Given a piecewise-smooth path $C$ and a vector field \n\n$$\nF(x,y,z)=P(x,y,z)\\,i+Q(x,y,z)\\,j+R(x,y,z)\\,k\n$$\n\ndefined on $C,$ we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r={\\int }_{C}P(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}x+Q(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}y+R(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}z.\n$$\n\nTherefore, in our case, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{C}4\\,\\text{d}x+xz\\,\\text{d}y+y\\,\\text{d}z.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186600360 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "047015d3-245d-57d8-9e76-8638640ecadb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186599996} {:answer/id #uuid "1739086c-31bb-5871-a79e-7dc7695e0ae5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186600014} {:answer/id #uuid "2cba192d-7fa8-592e-8135-fb3dd84b80b3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "11" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186600042} {:answer/id #uuid "315ab749-9806-5ebd-8701-231f7b3657cd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186600049} {:answer/id #uuid "3be5ac37-072b-53ef-b802-b3646a736a0e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186600058}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186599996} :answer-field/id #uuid "b2bbd54c-6dcc-55cc-991a-9fa16098a05c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186600319}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "388215dc-389a-56fb-9d33-97db6c17cf42" :question/math-academy-id "q-18249" :question/problem "Given that\n$$\n\\displaystyle D=\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 6 & 2 \\\\ -8 & 0 & 5 \\\\ 3 & 1 & x\\end{bmatrix}\n$$ \nand $det(D)=481,$ find the value of $x.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's use our formula to find an expression for the determinant in terms of $x:$\n    \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{det}(D) & =\\begin{vmatrix}5 & 6 & 2 \\\\ -8 & 0 & 5 \\\\ 3 & 1 & x\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =5\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 5 \\\\ 1 & x\\end{vmatrix}-6\\begin{vmatrix}-8 & 5 \\\\ 3 & x\\end{vmatrix}+2\\begin{vmatrix}-8 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =5\\cdot (-5)-6\\cdot (-8x-15)+2\\cdot (-8) \\\\  & =-25+48x+90-16 \\\\  & =48x+49\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the determinant of the matrix is $481,$ we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}48x+49 & =481 \\\\ 48x & =432 \\\\ x & =9\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186600364 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "17aeb05a-e487-518c-9630-a0c653f6b51d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186600016} {:answer/id #uuid "3d1af278-e8e4-52d2-9804-634ea872c17d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "21" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186600061} {:answer/id #uuid "403668b1-8fb4-58d8-975d-0e843d0e3847" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "15" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186600071} {:answer/id #uuid "cc90f7f2-e51e-5277-a583-9ab101ca0841" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186600217} {:answer/id #uuid "d4eba8c2-f57a-5df1-92bb-22f7bf1452ae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186600226}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186600061} :answer-field/id #uuid "9cbb4232-604c-56ec-bd98-4cfc5b3af773" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186600313}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "59f080ed-4000-5ac9-baa7-abbf54f73867" :question/math-academy-id "q-38177" :question/problem "Find the value of $\\begin{vmatrix}1 & -1 & 3 \\\\ 5 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 0\\end{vmatrix}.$" :question/worked-solution "Using the formula, we take the alternating sum of the entries in the top row of the matrix, multiplied by their respective minors, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{vmatrix}1 & -1 & 3 \\\\ 5 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 0\\end{vmatrix} & =1\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{vmatrix}-(-1)\\begin{vmatrix}5 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{vmatrix}+3\\begin{vmatrix}5 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =1(0-0)+1(0-0)+3(5+2) \\\\  & =3\\cdot 7 \\\\  & =21\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186600391 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "21fb5c81-e911-5047-92b4-9100c681938e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-38" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186600027} {:answer/id #uuid "22e1e0a5-3267-5a45-84e5-4d4eb0d385b3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186600029} {:answer/id #uuid "7b3c2ab4-1137-52c1-8113-5e9e5649c8df" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "38" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186600132} {:answer/id #uuid "ea497630-5954-5c98-80ad-e0c8606a6f33" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-19" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186600251} {:answer/id #uuid "fb1f046d-797c-51ab-b542-8afe2156e353" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "19" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186600268}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186600027} :answer-field/id #uuid "652a2152-b3ad-5de0-9bb1-fcdfed73af23" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186600302}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "c4e7803f-b8da-56a8-b5b3-61e6989763e1" :question/math-academy-id "q-9832" :question/problem "Find the value of $\\begin{vmatrix}6 & -1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 7 & 1 & 0\\end{vmatrix}.$" :question/worked-solution "Using the formula, we take the alternating sum of the entries in the top row of the matrix, multiplied by their respective minors, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{vmatrix}6 & -1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 7 & 1 & 0\\end{vmatrix} & =6\\begin{vmatrix}3 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{vmatrix}-(-1)\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ 7 & 0\\end{vmatrix}+2\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 3 \\\\ 7 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =6(0-0)+1(0-0)+2(2-21) \\\\  & =2\\cdot (-19) \\\\  & =-38\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186600397 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "088ca494-dbe0-58cc-a825-9b36f7352e7a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186600000} {:answer/id #uuid "18be830b-1ffb-5bf6-8377-7849fc987525" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186600017} {:answer/id #uuid "26c28228-3482-5c92-9704-6be2b24b3aba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186600032} {:answer/id #uuid "94dac9f4-9cec-5f26-a068-9bab6dec10a2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186600156} {:answer/id #uuid "b7f4e25f-e39c-59cb-8c19-a107f78ddf77" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186600190}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186600017} :answer-field/id #uuid "b385464f-8745-5852-b86e-bb292cb24e13" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186600320}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "e5d8fd25-145e-5cde-b46d-30f32d959af5" :question/math-academy-id "q-18274" :question/problem "Given that $B=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -3 & -1 \\\\ m & 1 & m \\\\ -1 & 4 & -2\\end{bmatrix}$ and $det(B)=-50,$ find the value of $m.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's use our formula to find an expression for the determinant in terms of $m:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{det}(B) & =\\begin{vmatrix}2 & -3 & -1 \\\\ m & 1 & m \\\\ -1 & 4 & -2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =2\\begin{vmatrix}1 & m \\\\ 4 & -2\\end{vmatrix}-(-3)\\begin{vmatrix}m & m \\\\ -1 & -2\\end{vmatrix}-1\\begin{vmatrix}m & 1 \\\\ -1 & 4\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =2\\cdot (-2-4m)+3\\cdot (-2m+m)-(4m+1) \\\\  & =-4-8m-3m-4m-1 \\\\  & =-5-15m\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the determinant of the matrix is $-50,$ we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-5-15m & =-50 \\\\ -15m & =-45 \\\\ m & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186600801 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "150d882c-8eca-5b3a-904d-8847552b0a76" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186600525} {:answer/id #uuid "2013d3de-5742-5692-ac57-f557e1c182a5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186600532} {:answer/id #uuid "58441d9b-c9b7-5909-b862-0254b453474b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186600577} {:answer/id #uuid "ac693892-2021-599d-ac57-3f2f0e1ed7fc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186600637} {:answer/id #uuid "c62acbf3-d099-5a0b-a584-a351c1513e0a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186600658}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186600658} :answer-field/id #uuid "8f253fac-9f0a-5bd1-b395-b73eb0c278a9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186600751}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "759808a8-2eeb-533d-a63b-1bd6d07795c2" :question/math-academy-id "q-1343" :question/problem "What is the value of $y$ given that $a=⟨3,2⟩,\\,b=⟨4,y⟩$ and $a⊥b?$" :question/worked-solution "We know that $a⊥b$ ($a$ and $b$ are perpendicular) if and only if $a\\cdot b=0.$\n\nComputing the dot product, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\cdot b & ={a}_{x}\\cdot {b}_{x}+{a}_{y}\\cdot {b}_{y} \\\\  & =3\\cdot 4+2\\cdot y \\\\  & =12+2y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting the dot product equal to $0,$ we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\cdot b & =0 \\\\ 12+2y & =0 \\\\ 2y & =-12 \\\\ y & =-6\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186600819 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fdcc7075-f562-565c-9a88-9d71dde8daf7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186600703}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186600703} :answer-field/id #uuid "52d3a576-5a8d-5028-9735-82b7fed81d92" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186600730}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "b4bd4e66-06f4-5d64-b545-b7176da8b445" :question/math-academy-id "q-317916" :question/problem "If $\\overset{\\to }{k}=⟨-5,2⟩$ and $\\overset{\\to }{l}=⟨2,7⟩,$ then $\\overset{\\to }{k}\\cdot \\overset{\\to }{l}=${{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "Summing up the products of corresponding components, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{k}\\cdot \\overset{\\to }{l} & ={k}_{x}\\cdot {l}_{x}+{k}_{y}\\cdot {l}_{y} \\\\  & =-5\\cdot 2+2\\cdot 7 \\\\  & =-10+14 \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186600824 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "09504fd1-25ba-58b1-b06c-5d1f18823512" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186600514} {:answer/id #uuid "2c13ae37-ee70-5ac8-98cb-3396a76feefd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186600542} {:answer/id #uuid "661a4311-67f7-5e36-b79f-9ab8384ac9af" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "15" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186600586} {:answer/id #uuid "6bedfd4f-6d26-57f0-8d2e-ef755390fb99" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "21" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186600589} {:answer/id #uuid "d3eb596e-6e58-5e24-a984-09677c4dc418" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186600667}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186600667} :answer-field/id #uuid "201e20e1-d402-5d98-bc2a-11417ae045fe" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186600711}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "c5fbfcc2-b949-53d2-98e2-50b70397098b" :question/math-academy-id "q-101405" :question/problem "Let $a=⟨-7,2⟩$ and $b=⟨0,-3⟩.$ Find $a\\cdot b.$" :question/worked-solution "Summing up the products of corresponding components, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\cdot b & ={a}_{x}\\cdot {b}_{x}+{a}_{y}\\cdot {b}_{y} \\\\  & =(-7)\\cdot 0+2\\cdot (-3) \\\\  & =0-6 \\\\  & =-6\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186602426 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "47f78180-a08d-5661-bbe4-d0a2baea58a0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186602119} {:answer/id #uuid "8bf2d443-d588-5203-84a6-d78692e4bada" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186602183} {:answer/id #uuid "9e7f457c-70da-53f0-abfb-cee84174fbe8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186602203} {:answer/id #uuid "f3e6ef15-6402-5ca9-8078-d7147d1ee25a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186602293} {:answer/id #uuid "fb0df071-3e6c-5f63-b2ba-30a4163c9b10" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "None of the given regions is simply connected" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186602300}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186602293} :answer-field/id #uuid "1eb0df24-b5be-570d-a590-2c48ccd094b5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186602316}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "7bf0193e-b0ea-5d23-a23b-d2428a7d4bf0" :question/math-academy-id "q-123849" :question/problem "Which of the following regions are simply connected?\n\nI. ${R}^{2}$\n\nII. $\\{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,|\\,(x,y)\\ne (0,0)\\}$\n\nIII. $\\{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,|\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}>9\\}$" :question/worked-solution "A connected region $D$ is simply connected if every simple closed curve in $D$ can be contracted to a point.\n\n![](images/7d/7d6aea1a62fb290702630ef95ec86f4119e7c8fdd74b8148b62b66e5ac871b58.png)\n\nA connected *plane* region $D$ is simply connected if every simple closed curve in $D$ encloses only points in $D.$ In other words, the region does not contain any holes. Plane regions containing holes are not simply connected, since some simple closed curves cannot be contracted to a point:\n\n![](images/c3/c3c3e7a60ff0dd917cf6c0b293dae7510e457a0455629add1c54cadac65248b7.png)\n\nLet's now examine our regions in turn.\n\n- Region I is simply connected. It's a plane region without holes.\n\n- Region II is *not* simply connected since it's a plane region with a hole at $(0,0).$\n\n- Region III is *not* simply connected since it's a plane region with a hole. In this case, the hole contains all points on the disc ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\le 9.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186602429 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "32ec7652-5b57-5f72-ac42-1fd503fa8af0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186602105} {:answer/id #uuid "61f7617e-dc2d-5fcb-9681-ed7752309ab0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186602140} {:answer/id #uuid "7c5af136-90ba-5cee-bb9e-53ca8e710af5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186602161} {:answer/id #uuid "841c40f1-1c45-5e82-8b24-2363103a2399" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186602170} {:answer/id #uuid "e2c450fc-689d-5fb2-8f07-c191a91abe9f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186602276}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186602140} :answer-field/id #uuid "440a3dfa-4d37-52da-9b9f-31326eaff272" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186602329}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "809a490a-e7dc-5d97-b48f-6d169e3a5188" :question/math-academy-id "q-124779" :question/problem "Which of the following regions are simply connected?\n\nI. $\\{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,|\\,y+x\\ge 0\\}$\n\nII. $\\{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,|\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}>1\\}$\n\nIII. $\\{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,|\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}>0\\}$" :question/worked-solution "A connected region $D$ is simply connected if every simple closed curve in $D$ can be contracted to a point.\n\n![](images/7d/7d6aea1a62fb290702630ef95ec86f4119e7c8fdd74b8148b62b66e5ac871b58.png)\n\nA connected *plane* region $D$ is simply connected if every simple closed curve in $D$ encloses only points in $D.$ In other words, the region does not contain any holes. Plane regions containing holes are not simply connected, since some simple closed curves cannot be contracted to a point:\n\n![](images/c3/c3c3e7a60ff0dd917cf6c0b293dae7510e457a0455629add1c54cadac65248b7.png)\n\nLet's now examine our regions in turn.\n\n- Region I is simply connected. Indeed, it is the half-plane $y\\ge -x.$ So,   it's a plane region without holes.\n\n- Region II is *not* simply connected since it's a plane region with a hole (containing all points of the circle ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\le 1$).\n\n- Region III is *not* simply connected since it's a plane region with a hole at $(0,0).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186602449 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "765529ed-f240-5bdd-9d25-b58473dbdf6d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186602155} {:answer/id #uuid "7d0caacd-2bfe-5e6a-9eff-51243e95e523" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186602163} {:answer/id #uuid "898e92f0-2667-5184-98a1-5fc040445213" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186602179} {:answer/id #uuid "8bc37475-f05d-50ea-aec9-828117330508" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186602182} {:answer/id #uuid "efa444e8-27ba-54f6-bc0e-6112ed4aff7e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186602289}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186602289} :answer-field/id #uuid "b5d1fce3-1642-5b33-b2ea-ead7b1c59135" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186602369}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "d6d54c83-1e5c-5ee3-a1ac-7d742cfe58ec" :question/math-academy-id "q-125503" :question/problem "![](images/ba/bae5904772e7ba190a516cbe66839f0d2e38775dfdcad836314d5945bfd215ad.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F(x,y)$ shown above, and let $C$ be the path where the curve is traversed from $A$ to $B.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0\n$$\n\nII. $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=0\n$$\n\nIII. $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r\n$$ gives the total work done by a force $F$ in moving a particle along $C:$\n\n- If the force works *with* the motion of the particle (i.e., the force helps to push the particle along the path), then $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0.\n$$\n\n- If the force works *against* the motion  of the particle (i.e., the force impedes the motion of the particle as it moves along the path), then $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r<0.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement.\n\n- Statement I is false, while statement II is true. Since the force $F$ acts perpendicular to the motion of the particle as it moves from $A$ to $B,$ it neither helps nor hinders its motion. \n\n![](images/1b/1b22d5486ce7cd4ed083842ba019b454ff711f9fc5b50718b97e58e95ce647ad.png)\n\n \nSo, $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=0.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. If we reverse the orientation of $C,$ then the situation with regards to the work done by $F$ is similar to the previous case.\n\n![](images/4f/4f2ae4819d2534d2e6abfbfe4a2842597f1ad0845f7925f5f74c359b69f7ba1f.png)\n\n \nSo, $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186602460 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "27af338f-b10d-5fb4-a5e7-eb812dac37d4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I and IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186602092} {:answer/id #uuid "27cc20b1-ed4e-57b2-b908-adc9d7204452" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II, III, and IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186602093} {:answer/id #uuid "725cadcc-f8fc-56a7-af8c-d8da28ecc9d9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186602151} {:answer/id #uuid "8624ee8f-70af-5368-a536-eca4a665d021" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I, III, and IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186602174} {:answer/id #uuid "b24e014f-ebf5-5470-aa55-224a7e542145" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186602223}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186602174} :answer-field/id #uuid "2175eeea-277a-5b5a-b27e-7084d0976da3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186602319}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "f940c6ab-7660-5234-9b2d-cd084f12839e" :question/math-academy-id "q-98693" :question/problem "![](images/ae/ae00d26b2f31ca96aa75f15dd285b3cd8e6afc71d2f326927adeb7b6ce55589b.png)\n\nWhich of the regions shown above are simply connected?" :question/worked-solution "A connected region $D$ is simply connected if every simple closed curve in $D$ can be contracted to a point.\n\n![](images/7d/7d6aea1a62fb290702630ef95ec86f4119e7c8fdd74b8148b62b66e5ac871b58.png)\n\nA connected *plane* region $D$ is simply connected if every simple closed curve in $D$ encloses only points in $D.$ In other words, the region does not contain any holes. Plane regions containing holes are not simply connected, since some simple closed curves cannot be contracted to a point:\n\n![](images/c3/c3c3e7a60ff0dd917cf6c0b293dae7510e457a0455629add1c54cadac65248b7.png)\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the regions:  \n\n- Region I is simply connected. This is a connected region that does not contain holes.\n\n- Region II is not simply connected. Instead, this is a connected region that contains a hole.\n\n- Region III is simply connected. This is a connected region that does not contain holes.\n\n- Region IV is simply connected. This is a connected region that does not contain holes.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, III, and IV only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186603001 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4f673a98-edf8-54f3-948a-277dd1e6329c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186602657} {:answer/id #uuid "57dadcf0-f5de-5f7b-955e-8e12a371b619" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{2}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186602667} {:answer/id #uuid "6b828551-330b-5b84-94ae-ea6467e7ee90" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186602686} {:answer/id #uuid "c301459d-414f-50e1-bed1-e0d345a23956" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{8}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186602792} {:answer/id #uuid "c869e162-5555-59e7-be7a-3abc92994583" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{8}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186602801}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186602667} :answer-field/id #uuid "cf2b29e0-c376-5148-a245-225b8ede9e4d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186602965}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "11dbabc0-7711-573b-b0b4-90ec778ecbc8" :question/math-academy-id "q-101530" :question/problem "Let $c=⟨4,2,\\sqrt{5}⟩$ and $d=⟨-2,0,\\sqrt{5}⟩.$ Find ${\\text{comp}}_{d}\\,c-{\\text{comp}}_{c}\\,d.$" :question/worked-solution "Using the formula for the scalar projection, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{comp}}_{d}\\,c & =\\frac{c\\cdot d}{|\\,d\\,|} \\\\  & =\\frac{{c}_{x}{d}_{x}+{c}_{y}{d}_{y}+{c}_{z}{d}_{z}}{\\sqrt{{d}_{x}^{2}+{d}_{y}^{2}+{d}_{z}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{4\\cdot (-2)+2\\cdot 0+\\sqrt{5}\\cdot \\sqrt{5}}{\\sqrt{(-2)^{2}+{0}^{2}+{\\sqrt{5}}^{2}}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{9}} \\\\  & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{comp}}_{c}\\,d & =\\frac{c\\cdot d}{|\\,c\\,|} \\\\  & =\\frac{{c}_{x}{d}_{x}+{c}_{y}{d}_{y}+{c}_{z}{d}_{z}}{\\sqrt{{c}_{x}^{2}+{c}_{y}^{2}+{c}_{z}^{2}}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{{4}^{2}+{2}^{2}+{\\sqrt{5}}^{2}}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{25}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{comp}}_{d}\\,c-{\\text{comp}}_{c}\\,d & =-1-\\left(-\\frac{3}{5}\\right) \\\\  & =-\\frac{5}{5}+\\frac{3}{5} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186603016 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2660209c-3715-5973-bf4d-50a4f18cc2fa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "III and IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186602612} {:answer/id #uuid "3946c3b7-1b28-5c51-8ebb-c792ab9e72d2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186602632} {:answer/id #uuid "d46a5c1a-7b02-50c3-b4ce-744551a25f95" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186602823} {:answer/id #uuid "e7e2f754-d483-5299-b0f2-53392244945c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186602849} {:answer/id #uuid "ff5214d3-8fb3-5eda-8dd1-6d6839423eb0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I, II, and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186602872}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186602612} :answer-field/id #uuid "91e86b7d-c601-513d-8cbc-41f4dacb3f59" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186602945}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "262357b5-cdf6-5b17-b00b-c452d150325c" :question/math-academy-id "q-98692" :question/problem "![](images/b3/b34f89be650c6ef102c765c2ed5b07ad0e88d5cfe7506f199fe7760a7391dbb2.png)\n\nWhich of the regions shown above are simply connected?" :question/worked-solution "A connected region $D$ is simply connected if every simple closed curve in $D$ can be contracted to a point.\n\n![](images/7d/7d6aea1a62fb290702630ef95ec86f4119e7c8fdd74b8148b62b66e5ac871b58.png)\n\nA connected *plane* region $D$ is simply connected if every simple closed curve in $D$ encloses only points in $D.$ In other words, the region does not contain any holes. Plane regions containing holes are not simply connected, since some simple closed curves cannot be contracted to a point:\n\n![](images/c3/c3c3e7a60ff0dd917cf6c0b293dae7510e457a0455629add1c54cadac65248b7.png)\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the regions:   \n\n- Region I is not simply connected. Instead, this is a connected region that contains a hole.\n\n- Region II is not simply connected. Instead, this is a region that consists of two pieces (it's not connected).\n\n- Region III is simply connected. This is a connected region that does not contain holes.\n\n- Region IV is simply connected. This is a connected region that does not contain holes.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III and IV only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186603032 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "18f55394-bc06-51be-bada-b563124f812e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II and IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186602595} {:answer/id #uuid "5ad96096-f7c9-5e48-827c-1ad8473d624d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186602671} {:answer/id #uuid "6c74a174-9a93-5b1f-aae6-bfdfec4823c7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "III and IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186602688} {:answer/id #uuid "7b6b1e6f-9bc3-55cf-b0d0-f6f27d1b204e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186602702} {:answer/id #uuid "ab94e3ec-2cb9-5b7c-8ce2-99b0756e27d1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186602768}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186602702} :answer-field/id #uuid "63656f9d-99a3-5231-b25c-345a850d88b4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186602916}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "52635f81-a6f0-5c99-b137-563aeafcb009" :question/math-academy-id "q-123883" :question/problem "![](images/2c/2c74cb84bc71d656e198fe65a190135a0016fc0568f565e8072de886fcc6113c.png)\n\nWhich of the regions shown above are simply connected?" :question/worked-solution "A connected region $D$ is simply connected if every simple closed curve in $D$ can be contracted to a point.\n\n![](images/7d/7d6aea1a62fb290702630ef95ec86f4119e7c8fdd74b8148b62b66e5ac871b58.png)\n\nA connected *plane* region $D$ is simply connected if every simple closed curve in $D$ encloses only points in $D.$ In other words, the region does not contain any holes. Plane regions containing holes are not simply connected, since some simple closed curves cannot be contracted to a point:\n\n![](images/c3/c3c3e7a60ff0dd917cf6c0b293dae7510e457a0455629add1c54cadac65248b7.png)\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the regions:  \n\n- Region I is simply connected. This is a connected region that does not contain holes.\n\n- Region II is simply connected. This is a connected region that does not contain holes.\n\n- Region III is not simply connected. Instead, this is a connected region that contains a hole.\n\n- Region IV is not simply connected. Instead, this is a region that consists of two pieces (it's not connected).\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186603053 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d20f125b-7aff-53be-a9c9-d2e57bbc1f63" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186602818}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186602818} :answer-field/id #uuid "62f761c2-4b27-5a7f-a1d5-0ef876307182" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186602914}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "8ff967b1-896d-54c3-8f50-dfb6b29e1ce5" :question/math-academy-id "q-168042" :question/problem "Find the dot product of the vectors $b$ and $c.$\n\n$b\\cdot c=\\,$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The dot product of two vectors given in component form can be computed by summing up the products of corresponding components of vectors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}b\\cdot c & ={b}_{x}\\cdot {c}_{x}+{b}_{y}\\cdot {c}_{y}+{b}_{z}\\cdot {c}_{z}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom part 1, we have $b=⟨3,-3,-2⟩$ and $c=⟨3,3,3⟩.$ Therefore, the formula for the dot product gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}b\\cdot c & =3\\cdot 3+(-3)\\cdot 3+(-2)\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =9-9-6 \\\\  & =-6.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186603060 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "40a51686-e990-569c-b91a-8126a6715488" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186602640} {:answer/id #uuid "7c3ad4b5-14ed-5c8b-823b-716c21a1dd89" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186602703} {:answer/id #uuid "91cf0bfc-7786-57c5-bb04-92da6183b2d7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186602731} {:answer/id #uuid "c1c4d301-921f-53c6-8787-fc1c51f3ed25" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186602788} {:answer/id #uuid "f6ae5f4f-a17f-525b-84d0-859d6abab2b0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186602861}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186602703} :answer-field/id #uuid "f7d6f1e4-2dd4-5dc1-b015-de0b52fb344f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186602988}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "9e093f98-02e8-51c5-b590-443064b1b257" :question/math-academy-id "q-124772" :question/problem "Which of the following regions are simply connected?\n\nI. $\\{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,|\\,2{x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}\\ne 1\\}$\n\nII. $\\{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,|\\,y\\ge 0\\}$\n\nIII. $\\{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,|\\,(x,y)\\ne (1,0)\\}$" :question/worked-solution "A connected region $D$ is simply connected if every simple closed curve in $D$ can be contracted to a point.\n\n![](images/7d/7d6aea1a62fb290702630ef95ec86f4119e7c8fdd74b8148b62b66e5ac871b58.png)\n\nA connected *plane* region $D$ is simply connected if every simple closed curve in $D$ encloses only points in $D.$ In other words, the region does not contain any holes. Plane regions containing holes are not simply connected, since some simple closed curves cannot be contracted to a point:\n\n![](images/c3/c3c3e7a60ff0dd917cf6c0b293dae7510e457a0455629add1c54cadac65248b7.png)\n\nLet's now examine our regions in turn.\n\n- Region I is *not* simply connected. The region contains all the points of ${R}^{2}$ except those on the ellipse $2{x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}=1.$ Thus, the region is formed by two disjoint pieces and, therefore, is not even a connected region.\n\n- Region II  is simply connected. It's a plane region without holes.\n\n- Region III is *not* simply connected since it's a plane region with a hole at $(1,0).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186604575 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "70508425-a546-5585-aa5b-f04d5e4b0d5c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186604334}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186604334} :answer-field/id #uuid "d73b8dd5-c9df-5201-90cc-b94981794c84" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186604545}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "263f92f1-95f6-5b12-80cc-0dd93e921496" :question/math-academy-id "q-168028" :question/problem "Find the dot product of the vectors $u$ and $w.$\n\n$u\\cdot w=\\,$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The dot product of two vectors given in component form can be computed by summing up the products of corresponding components of vectors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u\\cdot w & ={u}_{x}\\cdot {w}_{x}+{u}_{y}\\cdot {w}_{y}+{u}_{z}\\cdot {w}_{z}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom part 1, we have $u=⟨2,2,4⟩$ and $w=⟨0,0,2⟩.$ Therefore, the formula for the dot product gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u\\cdot w & =2\\cdot 0+2\\cdot 0+4\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =0+0+8 \\\\  & =8.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186608215 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "15a61ad7-79c1-597e-b11c-19dd09903bcc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{x{e}^{6x}}{6}-\\frac{{e}^{6x}}{36}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186607829} {:answer/id #uuid "371c726f-d93e-535e-8a96-08a8cf95914f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{x{e}^{6x}}{6}+\\frac{{e}^{6x}}{6}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186607867} {:answer/id #uuid "e977e102-a296-5ad5-ac58-227bccc1a06d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{x{e}^{6x}}{6}-\\frac{{e}^{6x}}{6}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186608060} {:answer/id #uuid "ed1ae4d5-c8d6-5a8d-84e5-ae8b3f0a3d82" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{{e}^{6x}}{6}-\\frac{x{e}^{6x}}{36}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186608064} {:answer/id #uuid "fc91c68d-0d4b-50b6-a510-3e9c2f09fe7d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{{e}^{6x}}{6}-\\frac{{e}^{6x}}{36}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186608085}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186607829} :answer-field/id #uuid "18e8c34f-a0b9-520d-9f5e-f104314fae97" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186608094}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "ad8a7bb6-4f28-5469-bc34-0461c89c315f" :question/math-academy-id "q-14454" :question/problem "$$\n\\displaystyle \\int x{e}^{6x}\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$" :question/worked-solution "To solve this integral, we use the by-parts formula, given by\n\n$$\n\\int u{v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x=uv-\\int v{u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nWe need to express $\\displaystyle \\int x{e}^{6x}\\text{d}x$ as $\\displaystyle \\int u{v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x.$ Therefore, we set\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=x\\, & ⟹\\,{u}^{′}=1 \\\\ {v}^{′}={e}^{6x}\\, & ⟹\\,v=\\frac{{e}^{6x}}{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute the above into the integration by parts formula, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int u{v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x & =uv-\\int v{u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int x\\cdot {e}^{6x}\\,\\text{d}x & =x\\cdot \\frac{{e}^{6x}}{6}-\\int \\frac{{e}^{6x}}{6}\\cdot 1\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we calculate the integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int x{e}^{6x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{x{e}^{6x}}{6}-\\frac{1}{6}\\int {e}^{6x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{x{e}^{6x}}{6}-\\frac{{e}^{6x}}{36}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186608222 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5c48dd4b-db1e-5aca-a708-9c53f96b5c58" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{5(2{e}^{3}+1)}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186607924}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186607924} :answer-field/id #uuid "a779df38-7a72-5f60-a56e-44e357815ee8" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186608136}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "c270c805-bb9a-5905-914b-1f5a0872a942" :question/math-academy-id "q-349327" :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{1}5x{e}^{3x}\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Let's use integration by parts. We set\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=5x & ⇒ & {u}^{′}=5 \\\\ {v}^{′}={e}^{3x} & ⇒ & v=\\frac{1}{3}{e}^{3x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the integration by parts formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int u{v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x & =uv-\\int v{u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int 5x\\cdot {e}^{3x}\\,\\text{d}x & =5x\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}{e}^{3x}-\\int \\frac{1}{3}{e}^{3x}\\cdot 5\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}5x{e}^{3x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{5}{3}x{e}^{3x}\\,{|}_{0}^{1}-\\frac{5}{3}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{e}^{3x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{3}x{e}^{3x}\\,{|}_{0}^{1}-\\frac{5}{9}{e}^{3x}\\,{|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{3}({e}^{3}-0)-\\frac{5}{9}({e}^{3}-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{3}{e}^{3}-\\frac{5}{9}({e}^{3}-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{5(2{e}^{3}+1)}{9}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186613214 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "226e5c08-5e90-54e8-ba55-94ff70a2918d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186612636} {:answer/id #uuid "622cc9e2-2ef6-5d02-946e-267a859ae820" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186612755} {:answer/id #uuid "77ac1ae1-a299-53b3-80df-b73c63f68172" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186612792} {:answer/id #uuid "b6adf92e-cb25-50ee-a682-112f195d6fe6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186612895} {:answer/id #uuid "ba0e5afd-7ee3-585d-8784-da4b915162f5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186612898}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186612895} :answer-field/id #uuid "2947ec3e-35aa-5ec6-8ccf-8bea650fdaaf" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186613037}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "f23ad49e-ef92-5ef1-81e1-83a5c66a2e4a" :question/math-academy-id "q-129328" :question/problem "Find $\\lim_{x\\to (-1,-1)}x\\sqrt{{y}^{2}+1}.$" :question/worked-solution "Let the function $f(x,y)$ be defined as\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=x\\sqrt{{y}^{2}+1}.\n$$\n\nThe function $f(x,y)$ is continuous for all values of $x$ and $y.$ So, we can calculate the limit by substituting $(-1,-1)$ into the function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to (-1,-1)}x\\sqrt{{y}^{2}+1} & =(-1)\\sqrt{(-1)^{2}+1} \\\\  & =-\\sqrt{1+1} \\\\  & =-\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186615399 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "702fa4f4-f518-5942-b6df-d9f23876e98b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186614935} {:answer/id #uuid "70edf502-04d2-575b-809f-2d2b3bf30039" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "i-j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186614936} {:answer/id #uuid "8ca0aa1c-cc13-50cf-b91e-a6d27c8d6b05" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "i+j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186614991} {:answer/id #uuid "de1cab00-311b-5a6e-aff4-95a47690b0ff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "i" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186615135} {:answer/id #uuid "fbb72731-0954-5b72-b05b-912bed4ed70d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}(i+j)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186615193}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186614935} :answer-field/id #uuid "f0d417e8-34c5-5bdf-8c21-2d1818e98964" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186615345}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "6a158751-6d6d-5cc0-ad4e-3f464be9bff1" :question/math-academy-id "q-71376" :question/problem "Find a unit normal vector to the level curve of the function $f(x,y)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y$ that passes through the point where $(x,y)=(0,0).$" :question/worked-solution "A normal vector to the level curve that passes through the point where $(x,y)=(0,0)$, can be found by computing $∇f(0,0).$ \n\nSo, we first find the gradient of $f(x,y)$ and evaluate it at $(x,y)=(0,0):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y)\\text{ }i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y)\\text{ }j \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\text{ }i+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y\\text{ }j \\\\ ∇f(0,0) & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡0\\operatorname{sin}⁡0\\text{ }i+\\operatorname{cos}⁡0\\operatorname{cos}⁡0\\text{ }j \\\\  & =j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we normalize $∇f(0,0):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∇f(0,0)}{‖∇f(0,0)‖} & =\\frac{j}{1}=j\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186615429 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0fa19e48-cf86-5e24-8f77-c9251f297b14" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}(-i+\\,j)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186614751} {:answer/id #uuid "28252441-6cf7-5f17-b6fd-fb7a87748417" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186614796} {:answer/id #uuid "2f300b0f-c4aa-568a-b76c-8de7a4158b00" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{5}(2\\,i-\\,j)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186614815} {:answer/id #uuid "af8e1d86-1d2f-5e39-b1d5-a23a3f4c6889" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "i" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186615057} {:answer/id #uuid "d8a1b66b-dc2b-59e8-970a-84f8410c77a0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{5}(i-2\\,j)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186615126}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186615126} :answer-field/id #uuid "76fe7a72-7549-5000-a49e-e35f709a6acc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186615274}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "940b6840-d17a-5dc4-bafd-7f85cea2e772" :question/math-academy-id "q-129911" :question/problem "Find a unit normal vector to the level curve of the function $f(x,y)={x}^{2}y-x{y}^{3}$ that passes through the point where $(x,y)=(1,1).$" :question/worked-solution "A normal vector to the level curve that passes through the point $(x,y)=(1,1)$ can be found by computing $∇f(1,1).$ \n\nSo, we first find the gradient and evaluate it at $(x,y)=(1,1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & ={f}_{x}(x,y)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y)\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}y-x{y}^{3})\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}y-x{y}^{3})\\,j \\\\  & =(2xy-{y}^{3})\\,i+({x}^{2}-3x{y}^{2})\\,j \\\\ ∇f(1,1) & =(2(1)(1)-{1}^{3})\\,i+({1}^{2}-3(1)(1)^{2})\\,j \\\\  & =i-2\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we normalize $∇f(1,1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∇f(1,1)}{‖∇f(1,1)‖} & =\\frac{\\,i-2\\,j}{\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+(-2)^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{5}(\\,i-2\\,j)\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186616295 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2ce4232b-b178-5d4f-8204-e1ac0f1ca5c0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-108" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186616289} {:answer/id #uuid "697870bd-dba1-58d7-bc06-c17d2efc7898" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "24" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186616298} {:answer/id #uuid "85a48f00-889f-5df2-afdf-9ae6a0bbb19c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "79" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186616302} {:answer/id #uuid "e505b4d0-1f6a-5d07-9b69-154f0ead16b9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186616315} {:answer/id #uuid "fb3aba9f-0a4e-5652-8c50-6c9034dbd972" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "17" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186616320}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186616302} :answer-field/id #uuid "41d621b2-0064-54ca-8333-6c9f13a931f4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186616291}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "5989794c-3519-5c45-9b2f-39f8c6d1c29d" :question/problem "Let $\\overset{\\to }{u}=⟨-3,11,2⟩$ and $\\overset{\\to }{v}=⟨5,10,-8⟩.$ Find $\\overset{\\to }{u}\\cdot \\overset{\\to }{v}.$" :question/worked-solution "Summing up the products of corresponding components, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{u}\\cdot \\overset{\\to }{v} & ={u}_{x}\\cdot {v}_{x}+{u}_{y}\\cdot {v}_{y}+{u}_{z}\\cdot {v}_{z} \\\\  & =(-3)\\cdot 5+11\\cdot 10+2\\cdot (-8) \\\\  & =-15+110-16 \\\\  & =79\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186616296 :question/answer-fields [{: